<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John, Margrave of Brandenburg-Küstrin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1513">1513</a>–<a href="/years/1571">1571</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Hans of Küstrin, John of Küstrin · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">House of Hohenzollern</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John, Margrave of Brandenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kostrzyn-nad-odra">Küstrin</a>, known as Hans of Küstrin, was a younger son of Elector Joachim I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> who received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neumark">Neumark</a> east of the Oder in 1535. He introduced the Lutheran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in 1538, turned Küstrin into a strong fortress and ran a thrifty, efficient government. He died without sons, and his lands returned to Brandenburg.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-margrave-of-brandenburg-kustrin.jpg" alt="John, Margrave of Brandenburg-Küstrin" width="320" height="458" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JohannBrandenburgK%C3%BCstrin.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>John was born on 3 August 1513, the younger son of Joachim I Nestor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> and Elizabeth of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a>, and a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-hohenzollern">House of Hohenzollern</a>. His father's will divided the country between him and his elder brother, Joachim II, who inherited the electoral title. When Joachim I died on 11 July 1535, John received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neumark">New March</a> with Dramburg and Schivelbein, the Sternberg Land, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krosno-odrzanskie">Crossen an der Oder</a> with Züllichau and Sommerfeld, and the lordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cottbus">Cottbus</a> with Peitz, about 12,500 square kilometres in all. In 1537 he married Duchess Catherine of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a> at Wolfenbüttel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Unlike his extravagant brother, John was deeply religious and had a good grasp of economic and political affairs. He joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-league">Schmalkaldic League</a> in 1538, yet in the Schmalkaldic War he fought on the side of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>, hoping to gain the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a>; the emperor granted him only freedom of religion in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neumark">New March</a>. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a> in 1548 he rejected the Augsburg Interim and refused to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corpus-christi-feast">Corpus Christi</a> procession. He escaped arrest through the intercession of King Ferdinand and because his brother was an Elector, and at home he never officially published the Interim.</p><p>He strengthened his defences and planned an anti-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> alliance that never came about. After a dispute with Elector Maurice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> at Annaburg in 1551 he stayed out of the princes' league against the Interim, refused the advances of Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-duke-of-prussia">Albert of Prussia</a>, and did not press his claim to Brandenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kulmbach">Kulmbach</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Joachim II died on 3 January 1571, and John died ten days later, on 13 January. He was buried in a crypt under the altar of St. Mary's church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kostrzyn-nad-odra">Küstrin</a>. His margraviate passed back to the March of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> under his nephew John George. John left a fortune of more than half a million guilders, while his brother left debts of 2.5 million. Of his two daughters, Elisabeth married to Margrave George Frederick I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-ansbach">Brandenburg-Ansbach</a> and Catherine married Elector Joachim Frederick of Brandenburg.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John, Margrave of Brandenburg-Küstrin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Margrave John, known as <b>Hans of Küstrin</b>, introduced Lutheranism in 1538, ahead of his brother.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-brandenburg-kustrin">Margraviate of Brandenburg-Küstrin</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Tangermünde, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kostrzyn-nad-odra">Kostrzyn nad Odrą</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=52.54,11.97&amp;year=1550" aria-label="Tangermünde on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4818 351 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John, Margrave of Brandenburg-Küstrin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5268 621h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5268 621h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=52.54,11.97&amp;year=1550">What was here in 1550? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
